# NestJS security

Stack-specific request-pipeline risks for a NestJS application — where an
unvalidated or unauthorized request can reach application code — narrowing
the OWASP-style guidance in the common rules. Applies only to `*.ts` files
in a NestJS project; SQL/ORM-specific injection risk is covered by the
`review-backend` skill and the project's ORM rules, not duplicated here.

## Input validation

- Every controller method that accepts a body, query, or route param has a
  typed DTO class validated by a global `ValidationPipe` (registered once,
  in `main.ts` via `app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe(...))`, or as an
  `APP_PIPE` provider) — a per-controller `@UsePipes(ValidationPipe)` that
  some controllers omit is the same gap as no `ValidationPipe` at all for
  the endpoints that skip it.
- Enable `whitelist: true` (strip unknown properties) and
  `forbidNonWhitelisted: true` (reject a request carrying an unknown
  property) on the global `ValidationPipe` — without them, a DTO's
  `class-validator` decorators only validate the fields they name; any
  extra field the client sends still reaches the handler untouched.
- Never trust a query/param value's type from the route alone — a route
  param typed `id: string` in the controller signature is still a raw
  string at runtime; convert and validate it (`ParseIntPipe`,
  `ParseUUIDPipe`, or a DTO with `@IsInt()`/`@IsUUID()`) rather than
  passing it straight to a repository call.

## AuthN/AuthZ

- Protect every route that must not be public with a guard
  (`@UseGuards(...)` or a global `APP_GUARD`); a route with neither a guard
  nor an explicit `@Public()`-style marker is not provably intentional —
  treat an undecorated route in an otherwise-guarded controller as a gap to
  confirm, not assume-safe.
- Read the authenticated principal from the guard-populated request object
  (e.g. a custom `@CurrentUser()` param decorator backed by
  `request.user`) — never from a client-supplied header, body field, or
  query param that claims who the caller is.
- A role/permission check belongs in a guard evaluated before the handler
  runs, not as an `if` inside the service after data has already been
  fetched or mutated.

## Response and error handling

- An exception filter (global `APP_FILTER`, or `app.useGlobalFilters(...)`)
  must translate an unexpected internal error into a generic response —
  never let a raw `error.message`/`error.stack` from an ORM, an HTTP
  client, or an uncaught exception reach the HTTP response body; that
  leaks internal schema, file paths, or connection details to the caller.
- Use NestJS's built-in `HttpException` subclasses (or your own subclass of
  `HttpException`) for expected error conditions so the status code and
  the response shape stay in the framework's own exception-filter
  pipeline, instead of manually setting `response.status(...)` in a
  controller and bypassing filters.

## Secrets and configuration

- Read secrets (API keys, DB credentials, JWT signing keys) through
  `ConfigService`/`@nestjs/config`, never hardcoded in a provider or
  committed default value — a `useFactory` provider that falls back to a
  literal secret when an env var is unset is the same risk as hardcoding
  it.
- Do not log a full request/response body in an interceptor or middleware
  applied globally without redacting known-sensitive DTO fields (passwords,
  tokens, card numbers) — a broad request logger is a common accidental
  secret-leak path into log storage.
